    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters May 29th with **James Wood** headlining the card as our definitive anchor. Sporting a robust 76.28 score, **James Wood** benefits from a high-upside matchup against Lucas Giolito, whose 5.37 xERA provides a green light for offensive output. When paired with the +19% SP skill boost and a projected leadoff spot, **James Wood** offers the highest ceiling for runs and RBI potential on the slate. Following closely are **Austin Riley** (66.29) and **Josh Naylor** (63.93), both of whom capitalize on favorable park factors and high-leverage order placement, despite the presence of formidable arms like Chris Paddack and Zac Gallen. We are also tracking **Corbin Carroll** (62.47) closely; while he faces an elite arm in George Kirby, his 87% stolen base threat against the pitcher's slow delivery makes him a mandatory start in our pursuit of category scarcity.\n\nThe most difficult roster decision today involves the final OF/UTIL spots, where **Zach McKinstry** (55.06) barely edged out **Vinnie Pasquantino** (46.58). While **Vinnie Pasquantino** offers a steady floor, he is hampered by a significant -15% dynamic platoon hit against the southpaw MacKenzie Gore, rendering his profile too stagnant for our current needs. Conversely, **Zach McKinstry** remains a high-variance target because of his 93% efficiency in the stolen base game against Erick Fedde, illustrating our organizational mandate to prioritize speed in tight rotisserie matchups. We must exercise caution with **Gabriel Moreno** and **Dalton Rushing**; both are dealing with elite pitchers and \"lineup pending\" tags, which, if confirmed in the bottom third of their respective orders, could see their efficiency scores dip further below their current marginal thresholds.",
